led slugs, and land snails that have only a very small shell (that they cannot retract into) are sometimes called semi-slugs."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-snail"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snail"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Moroccan cuisine","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine",[["Babbouche","dish/babbouche"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Anise","ingredient/anise"],["Elettaria cardamomum","ingredient/elettaria-cardamomum"],["Liquorice","ingredient/liquorice"],["Mentha pulegium","ingredient/mentha-pulegium"],["Ras el hanout","ingredient/ras-el-hanout"],["Thymus","ingredient/thymus"]]}]},"ingredient/namul":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/namul","n":"Namul","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Namul refers to either a variety of edible greens or leaves or seasoned herbal dishes made of them. Wild greens are called san-namul (산나물; lit. 'mountain namul'), and spring vegetables are called bom-namul (봄나물; lit. 'spring namul'). On the day of Daeboreum, the first full moon of the year, Koreans eat boreum-namul (보름나물; lit. 'full moon namul') with five-grain rice. It is believed that boreum namuls eaten in winter help one to withstand the heat of the summer to come."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-namul"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Namul"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Bibimbap","dish/bibimbap"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Gochujang","ingredient/gochujang"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]}]},"dish/burger-king-mattoon-illinois":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/burger-king-mattoon-illinois","n":"Burger King (Mattoon, Illinois)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Fast food","dot":"#a99fb5","intro":[["Burger King is a restaurant founded by the Hoots family and operated by Burger King LLC of Illinois in Mattoon, Illinois, United States; it is not related to the fast food chain Burger King. The restaurant's founders claim that it is the \"original\" Burger King and predates the fast food chain. A 1968 court case between this restaurant and the larger Burger King chain is a landmark case in United States trademark law regarding the Lanham Act."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-burger-king-mattoon-illinois"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burger_King_(Mattoon%2C_Illinois)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Fast food","cuisine/fast-food"]]},{"h":"More from Fast food","links":[["The Varsity (restaurant)","dish/the-varsity-restaurant"]]}]},"ingredient/shaved-ice":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/shaved-ice","n":"Shaved ice","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Shaved ice is a large family of ice-based desserts made of fine shavings of ice and sweet condiments or flavored syrups. Usually, the syrup is added after the ice has been frozen and shaved—typically at the point of sale; however, flavoring can also be added before freezing. The dessert is consumed worldwide in various forms and ways. Shaved ice can also be mixed with large quantities of liquid to produce shaved ice drinks."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-shaved-ice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaved_ice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Cendol","dish/cendol"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Ice","ingredient/ice"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"]]}]},"dish/chakapuli":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chakapuli","n":"Chakapuli","c":"#ede6da","cr